{"event": "Built docker exec command args=['docker', 'exec', '--workdir', '/workspace', '--user', '1000:1000', '--env', 'UV_CACHE_DIR=/tmp/uv-cache', '--env', 'PIP_CACHE_DIR=/tmp/pip-cache-dir', '--env', 'PIP_CONFIG_FILE=/tmp/pip-conf', '--env', 'HOME=/tmp/agent_home', '--env', 'CLAUDE_CODE_PRINT_BG_WAIT_CEILING_MS=0', '--env', 'CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=***redacted***', '--env', 'FORCE_AUTO_BACKGROUND_TASKS=1', '--env', 'ENABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS=1', '--env', 'CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_EXPERIMENTAL_BETAS=1', '--env', 'DISABLE_AUTOUPDATER=1', '--env', 'DISABLE_NON_ESSENTIAL_MODEL_CALLS=1', '--env', 'MAX_THINKING_TOKENS=31999', '--env', 'CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL=high', '--env', 'SLOP_EXEC_ID=bd4fc4ccf1bc48cca069660b5266f6fc', 'b52c1ebe0877bd9c2ed799db9461034799e6e40718f9d64517a69c055f58488a', '/bin/sh', '-c', 'claude --output-format stream-json --verbose --model claude-opus-5 --disallowedTools Monitor --permission-mode bypassPermissions --print -- \\'# Task Directions\\n\\nFully implement the following spec. Use the approach:\\n- Environment setup\\n- Write tests from the spec\\n- Implement\\n- Declare complete\\n\\nSpecifically:\\n\\n# Environment\\n\\nKeep using the same virtual environment you started with, update `requirements.txt` with any new dependencies you need.\\nTouch an IN_PROGRESS file. Delete any COMPLETED file.\\n\\nWhen killing a process never run a pkill. Instead, pgrep.\\nMake sure only one process is found, then kill that one by PID.\\nWhy? It\\'\"\\'\"\\'s too easy to pkill your own Bash argv.\\n\\nFor long-lived processes, log to a file or /dev/null instead of an undrained pipe.\\n\\n# Testing\\n\\nSplit the spec file into minimal possible logical phrases and minimal context, and iterate through each phrase.\\nFor each phrase, add a test section with a comment of the phrase & context.\\n\\nIf the spec is non-trivially under-specified, such that alternative interpretations would change the behavior of a conforming implementation on some input, then never ask for clarification.\\nInstead, make your best choice at how to interpret the intention of the spec.\\nThen, add a numbered entry to an AMBIGUITIES.md file, starting with T1.\\nThe entry must have sections for Spec Text, Alternatives, Choice, and Risk.\\nThe entry will quote the relevant spec source verbatim, explain the reasonable alternative interpretations, and your choice & why it seems to best fit the spec.\\nThe \"### Risk: N\" section\\nRisk is a number from 0 to 100: the chance that the spec\\'\"\\'\"\\'s author, who has an implementation of their own and writes the hidden tests against it, prefers a different reading from your choice on some tested input.\\nReason about what the author\\'\"\\'\"\\'s implementation most likely does and what fixture they would naturally write, not only about which reading the prose supports best.\\nIf Risk is above 0, name the reading the author most likely prefers in one line.\\nUse the whole range: a flat number on every entry is useless.\\n\\nIf a previous ambiguity is resolved by the current spec, annotate the entry instead of removing it.\\n\\nYou run tests only to validate the tests themselves: collection succeeds, helpers and generators work, and each failure is because behavior is missing, not because the test is broken. Quick, targeted runs.\\n\\nNever run the full suite to completion or long hypothesis runs; a green suite is the implementer\\'\"\\'\"\\'s job, not yours.\\n\\n# Implement\\n- Follow best coding practices:\\n  - Group functions into files based on related functionality\\n  - Keep your code clean\\n- No god functions/classes.\\n- Make sure the code is documented appropriately so that it is easy to pick up.\\n- Minimize the following gotchas:\\n  - Extra defensive checks or try/catch blocks that are abnormal.\\n  - Casts to get around type checking\\n  - Variables that are only used a single time after declaration.\\n  - Extra comments that a human wouldn\\'\"\\'\"\\'t add.\\n  - Trivial wrappers\\n  - Heavy nesting\\n  - If/Else ladders\\n  - A ton of helper methods\\n\\n# Declare complete\\n\\ntouch a COMPLETED file. rm the IN_PROGRESS file.\\n\\n# Spec\\n# Smart Output Formatting, JSON Export, and Multi-XPath\\n\\n## CLI Addition\\n\\n- `-j`, `--json`: JSON export mode for XML element results.\\n\\n## Output Precedence\\n\\nWhen multiple output flags are present:\\n\\n1. `--text-all`\\n2. `--text`\\n3. `--json`\\n4. default auto-format behavior\\n\\nNotes:\\n\\n- `--json` has no effect when results are already auto-detected as text\\n  (e.g., text()/attribute outputs).\\n- In `--css` mode, `--json` has no effect and output follows the normal CSS\\n  rules for the query.\\n\\n## JSON Export Contract\\n\\n- Export shape: JSON array of objects `{tag_name: immediate_text_content}`.\\n- Use immediate text only (exclude descendant text from child elements).\\n- Default JSON formatting: 2-space indent.\\n- With `--compact`, produce zero-indent JSON layout (no leading whitespace).\\n- With `--first`, still return a JSON array containing one exported element.\\n\\n## XPath Union\\n\\n- Support XPath `|` union in XPath mode.\\n- `--text` and `--text-all` apply across union queries.\\n- For XPath union queries with active `--text-all`, concatenate descendant text\\n  across all union matches into one normalized output string.\\n- If `--first` is also active with union + `--text-all`, only the first matched\\n  union node contributes to the output.\\n- If any union sub-path already performs text extraction, text flags are treated as no-op query modifiers.\\n- In CSS mode, pass `|` through as CSS query text (no XPath union semantics).\\n  If the CSS engine rejects the selector, report a CSS selector error on stderr\\n  with exit code `1`.\\'']", "timestamp": "2026-09-19 14:53:34", "level": "verbose", "process_id": 879730, "thread_name": "MainThread"}
